skill: SQL Joins and Aggregations

@ Inner and Outer Joins | B
Which join returns only the rows that have matching values in both tables?
= INNER JOIN
- LEFT JOIN
- CROSS JOIN
- FULL OUTER JOIN
? An INNER JOIN keeps a row only when the join condition matches in both tables.

@ Inner and Outer Joins | B
What does a RIGHT JOIN return?
= All rows from the right table and the matching rows from the left table
- All rows from the left table and the matching rows from the right table
- Only the rows that match in both tables
- Every combination of rows from both tables
? Rows of the right table with no match get NULL in the left table columns.

@ Inner and Outer Joins | I
What does this query return?
  SELECT c.name, o.id FROM customers c
  LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id
  WHERE o.id IS NULL;
= Customers who have never placed an order
- All orders with their customers
- Customers who have placed at least one order
- A syntax error
? The LEFT JOIN keeps all customers. Rows where o.id IS NULL are customers with no matching order.

@ Inner and Outer Joins | I
Which join type returns all rows from both tables, with NULLs where there is no match?
= FULL OUTER JOIN
- INNER JOIN
- CROSS JOIN
- NATURAL JOIN
? FULL OUTER JOIN combines LEFT and RIGHT JOIN results. MySQL has no FULL JOIN keyword, so it is emulated with UNION.

@ Inner and Outer Joins | I
In a JOIN query, what does the ON clause define?
= The condition used to match rows between the two tables
- The sort order of the result
- Which columns appear in the result
- How rows are grouped
? ON specifies how a row of one table relates to a row of the other, usually key equality.

@ Self and Cross Joins | I
Table A has 3 rows and table B has 4 rows. How many rows does A CROSS JOIN B return?
= 12
- 7
- 4
- 3
? A cross join produces the Cartesian product: 3 x 4 = 12 rows.

@ Self and Cross Joins | I
Which join helps you list each employee together with their manager when both are rows of the same employees table?
= Self join
- Cross join
- Natural join
- Full outer join
? A self join joins a table to itself using two aliases, for example employees e JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id.

@ Aggregation and Grouping | I
What does this query do?
  SELECT dept, AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept ORDER BY AVG(salary) DESC;
= Lists departments sorted from highest to lowest average salary
- Returns one row with the overall average salary
- Sorts departments alphabetically
- Raises an error because ORDER BY cannot use aggregates
? ORDER BY may use aggregate expressions when the query is grouped.

@ Aggregation and Grouping | I
Why does SELECT dept, name, COUNT(*) FROM emp GROUP BY dept; fail in strict SQL mode?
= name is neither in the GROUP BY clause nor inside an aggregate function
- COUNT(*) cannot be combined with GROUP BY
- dept is a reserved word
- GROUP BY requires a HAVING clause
? Every selected column must be grouped or aggregated, otherwise the value for each group is ambiguous.

@ Aggregation and Grouping | I
What does COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) return?
= The number of different customers
- The total number of rows
- The number of NULL values
- The sum of all customer ids
? DISTINCT inside COUNT counts each different non-NULL value once.

@ Aggregation and Grouping | A
Which query finds email addresses that appear more than once in a users table?
= SELECT email, COUNT(*) FROM users GROUP BY email HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;
- SELECT email, COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE COUNT(*) > 1 GROUP BY email;
- SELECT DISTINCT email FROM users;
- SELECT email FROM users ORDER BY COUNT(*) > 1;
? Group by email and use HAVING to keep only groups with more than one row. WHERE cannot filter on aggregates.

@ Set Operations | I
Which statement about UNION and UNION ALL is correct?
= UNION removes duplicate rows while UNION ALL keeps them
- UNION keeps duplicates while UNION ALL removes them
- They always return identical results
- UNION ALL sorts the combined result
? UNION performs an extra duplicate-elimination step, so UNION ALL is faster when duplicates do not matter.

@ Window Functions | A
Which function numbers rows 1, 2, 3 ... within each partition, giving every row a unique number?
= ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...)
- RANK() OVER (...)
- COUNT(*) GROUP BY ...
- SUM() OVER ()
? ROW_NUMBER always gives distinct sequential numbers. RANK gives tied rows the same number.

@ Window Functions | A
Salaries 100, 100 and 90 are ranked with RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC). Which ranks result?
= 1, 1, 3
- 1, 1, 2
- 1, 2, 3
- 1, 2, 2
? RANK gives ties the same rank and then skips the following rank. DENSE_RANK would return 1, 1, 2.
